00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.730 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in the summer of 2014 a streamer by the  name of Cosmo Wright made history  broadcasting out of his apartment in  Chicago Illinois he set a world record  by completing the Legend of Zelda  Ocarina of Time in 18 minutes and 10  seconds it was an incredible feat by  many standards beating a massive  adventure game like that in under 20  minutes simply doesn't make any sense to  the uninitiated but to the people who  knew him in his history with the game  cosmos record was a thing of legends by  employing a number of seemingly  impossible glitches and some incredibly  precise movement he had achieved a  near-perfect run and was able to shave  off almost a minutes worth of time from  the previous world record it was an  amazing performance by one of the  world's best speed runners but four  years prior to cosmos legendary run  something bigger was brewing something  that would change the way speed runs  were seen in the public eye and by the  people within the gaming world itself  speed running is an incredibly  fascinating niche within the gaming  world it challenges players to bend the  rules of their favorite games and to  challenge everything they know about how  they operate whether it's utilizing  glitches to skip entire areas abusing  existing mechanics or simply tearing  through the game on skill alone runners  are constantly finding new ways to reach  the credits screen pester time time  the way in which some games unravel  themselves after being split open can be  captivating experience breathing new  life into titles previously thought to  be boring or uninteresting into 2010 the  people over at speed demos archive a  website dedicated to speedrunning  brought that experience to the entire  world by gathering together volunteers  from across the country they starred  games done quick a biannual speedrun  marathon which donates all the money  they received during their week-long  live stream to charity to this date  they've raised over 10 million dollars  over the course of seven years and 15  separate events benefiting such causes  as the prevent Cancer Foundation and  Doctors Without Borders this marriage of  gaming and philanthropy is an inspiring  relationship that simply seeks to be a  positive force in the streaming world  and although games done 'quick may not  have been the first organization to  combine video games and charity their  unique take on the Union has proven to  be a successful  [Applause]  [Music]  most major gaming events in our current  age are influenced in some way by money  and to a greater extent the companies  that finance them watch any competitive  gaming event and it won't take you long  to identify the team sponsors but when  it comes to games done quick it's their  goodwill and humanitarianism that takes  center stage over product placement and  commercials there are slight nods to  their sponsors that help finance the  event but they stay true to their  grassroots origin by shining the  spotlight primarily on the people  playing the games and it's that passion  towards both the art of speedrunning and  the causes that they raise money for  that make their streams feel and daring  and their success feel earned but  charity isn't the only element of the  gaming world that speedrunning has  affected in some cases it's caused both  the players and the developers to  analyze game design more closely the  indie game dustforce is an example of  the harmony between the two parties with  its in-game timer  leaderboards and mechanics that reward  speed and precision the developers  themselves have said that they wanted to  make a game for people who cared about  mastery when players progress in  dustforce they are actually improving as  a player it's not any stats or items but  the players own ability that grows this  symbiotic relationship where the  developers are just as involved with the  game being played as the speedrunners  are is a bond that promotes growth among  everyone involved and on the other side  of that coin there are people who take  it upon themselves to dive deep into the  inner workings of the game to  deconstruct it and to break down the  barriers that separate us from the ones  and zeros in its code to me Mario Super  Mario 64 is a game that has led a  bountiful life since the 90s full of  people speedrunning the game and  perfecting its mechanics but the game  has taken on an even newer life thanks  in part to a recent exploit found that  involves what its founder calls half a  presses now the 30 minute video  explaining it gets really complex and  detailed with the narrator going on to  explain parallel universes within the  game but simply put the exploit uses a  number of glitches to have Mario build  speed over the course of 12 hours and  instantaneously launching them through  time and space to collect a level star  and although the glitch was  tool-assisted meaning the possibility of  a human recreating is next to zero it  still remains a testament to the  persistence of speedrunners and their  commitment to finding new things within  a game long after they've fallen out of  the public eye  speedrunning may typically be a solo  endeavor but if there's anything that  the countless marathons tool-assisted  runs and physics defying glitches I've  taught us it's that's also a community  effort the people taking part have  quickly turned it into something of a  fellowship one that fosters inclusion in  unity with its members in just a few  short years games done quick has  transformed into something much more  than just a speedrunning marathon it's  become a beacon of guidance for all  those interested in the craft a home for  the most weird and wonderful aspects of  gaming to be shown to the world and a  pillar upon which its members love for  both charity and video games are upheld  in champion cosmos record on that warm  July night was significant not only  because of how quickly he had beaten the  game but also because of how each  subsequent record that bested his own  echo the theme of camaraderie that the  speedrun community had established years  of for each new person to claim that top  spot benefited from the experience and  knowledge left behind by their  predecessor every new strategy or bug or  glitch  was shared with the understanding that  everyone was working towards the same  goal but speedrunning rarely if ever  feels like a competition as its appeal  goes beyond simply getting the fastest  time more than anything else it's about  challenging oneself testing one's own  endurance and ability to see the game  beyond a surface level much like the  games done quick events themselves speed  running feels less like a sprint and  more like a marathon because no matter  how fast or how slow your time is the  most important thing is that you make it  to the end with the love and support of  everyone waiting for you at the finish  line  [Music]

